1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,335
A road sign says: speed twenty
to forty kilometres per hour.

2
00:00:04,735 --> 00:00:07,078
Aarav is riding at exactly forty.

3
00:00:07,078 --> 00:00:10,228
Is he inside the limit,
or just outside it?

4
00:00:10,678 --> 00:00:13,808
He saw the sign on his
bicycle ride to college.

5
00:00:14,158 --> 00:00:16,021
Twenty to forty sounds clear.

6
00:00:16,021 --> 00:00:17,650
But it hides a question.

7
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:19,326
Are the two ends allowed?

8
00:00:19,826 --> 00:00:24,851
Maths answers it with one tiny detail:
a round bracket, or a square bracket.

9
00:00:24,851 --> 00:00:26,263
These are intervals.

10
00:00:26,913 --> 00:00:31,322
An interval is a set of real numbers
on the number line, between two ends,

11
00:00:31,322 --> 00:00:33,585
a and b, where a is less than b.

12
00:00:33,985 --> 00:00:36,461
It holds every real number in between.

13
00:00:36,461 --> 00:00:38,040
Not just whole numbers.

14
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,604
Twenty point five, thirty
one point two, all of them.

15
00:00:42,004 --> 00:00:47,114
So an interval is an infinite set, and
it is a subset of R, the real numbers.

16
00:00:47,764 --> 00:00:51,050
The open interval, a to
b, uses round brackets.

17
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:54,780
It is every x with a less
than x, and x less than b.

18
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:56,452
Both ends are left out.

19
00:00:56,852 --> 00:01:00,276
On the number line, we draw
its ends as hollow circles.

20
00:01:00,876 --> 00:01:04,396
The closed interval, a to
b, uses square brackets.

21
00:01:04,396 --> 00:01:06,585
Now a and b are both included.

22
00:01:06,585 --> 00:01:11,144
a is less than or equal to x,
and x is less than or equal to b.

23
00:01:11,544 --> 00:01:13,839
Its ends are drawn as filled dots.

24
00:01:14,439 --> 00:01:17,710
Mix the brackets, and you
get half open intervals.

25
00:01:17,710 --> 00:01:21,482
Square bracket a, round bracket
b includes a, but not b.

26
00:01:21,482 --> 00:01:24,367
Round a, square b includes b, but not a.

27
00:01:25,017 --> 00:01:28,820
Now watch a point move toward
the top end of two intervals.

28
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:33,595
In the closed interval twenty to forty,
the point reaches forty, and forty

29
00:01:33,595 --> 00:01:34,495
is in.

30
00:01:34,614 --> 00:01:38,566
In the open interval, the point
can get as close as it likes.

31
00:01:38,566 --> 00:01:40,116
Thirty nine point nine.

32
00:01:40,116 --> 00:01:41,960
Thirty nine point nine nine.

33
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:42,860
Still in.

34
00:01:43,135 --> 00:01:44,830
But forty itself is out.

35
00:01:44,830 --> 00:01:48,318
And there is no largest number
inside an open interval.

36
00:01:48,318 --> 00:01:50,954
Whatever you pick, there is one closer.

37
00:01:51,604 --> 00:01:52,696
Pause and think.

38
00:01:52,696 --> 00:01:56,614
Is two in the interval, round
bracket two, five, square bracket?

39
00:01:59,814 --> 00:02:00,714
No!

40
00:02:00,244 --> 00:02:02,799
The round bracket at two leaves two out.

41
00:02:02,799 --> 00:02:05,541
Five is in, because of the square bracket.

42
00:02:06,191 --> 00:02:08,382
Every interval also has a length.

43
00:02:08,382 --> 00:02:09,284
b minus a.

44
00:02:09,684 --> 00:02:13,843
The open interval two to five and
the closed interval two to five both

45
00:02:13,843 --> 00:02:15,103
have length three.

46
00:02:15,103 --> 00:02:18,125
Leaving out the ends does
not change the length.

47
00:02:18,725 --> 00:02:21,126
An interval can also run forever.

48
00:02:21,126 --> 00:02:26,107
Zero to infinity, with zero included,
is the set of all the non negative

49
00:02:26,107 --> 00:02:27,191
real numbers.

50
00:02:27,591 --> 00:02:31,078
Infinity is not a number,
so it can never be included.

51
00:02:31,078 --> 00:02:33,108
It always gets a round bracket.

52
00:02:33,708 --> 00:02:34,722
Remember this.

53
00:02:34,722 --> 00:02:37,014
A round bracket leaves the end out.

54
00:02:37,014 --> 00:02:38,965
A square bracket keeps it in.

55
00:02:39,665 --> 00:02:41,401
Now, one worked example.

56
00:02:41,751 --> 00:02:46,693
Write the set of all real x, where
minus five is less than x, and x is

57
00:02:46,693 --> 00:02:49,895
less than or equal to
seven, as an interval.

58
00:02:50,395 --> 00:02:51,295
Step one.

59
00:02:51,251 --> 00:02:55,449
Minus five comes with less
than, so minus five is left out.

60
00:02:55,449 --> 00:02:56,566
Round bracket.

61
00:02:57,316 --> 00:02:58,216
Step two.

62
00:02:58,151 --> 00:03:02,345
Seven comes with less than or
equal to, so seven is included.

63
00:03:02,345 --> 00:03:03,485
Square bracket.

64
00:03:04,235 --> 00:03:05,146
Step three.

65
00:03:05,146 --> 00:03:09,710
The interval is round bracket minus
five, comma seven, square bracket.

66
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:13,778
Its length is seven minus
minus five, which is twelve.

67
00:03:14,428 --> 00:03:15,894
Now, the exam trap.

68
00:03:16,244 --> 00:03:20,549
Many students write the closed interval
two to five as the set two, three,

69
00:03:20,549 --> 00:03:21,465
four, five.

70
00:03:21,865 --> 00:03:26,302
But an interval holds every real
number between its ends, like two point

71
00:03:26,302 --> 00:03:27,311
five and pi.

72
00:03:27,311 --> 00:03:29,623
That list holds only four integers.

73
00:03:29,623 --> 00:03:31,494
They are not the same set.

74
00:03:32,144 --> 00:03:33,764
Where is this asked?

75
00:03:33,764 --> 00:03:37,679
In PUC board exams, as write
these sets as intervals.

76
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:42,670
And in JEE Main, for example in two
thousand twenty, where one open or

77
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:44,899
closed end decided the answer.

78
00:03:45,499 --> 00:03:46,747
Exam level now.

79
00:03:46,747 --> 00:03:49,519
How many integers lie in this interval?

80
00:03:49,519 --> 00:03:50,820
Pause and solve.

81
00:03:56,220 --> 00:03:57,409
The answer is B.

82
00:03:57,409 --> 00:03:58,309
Seven.

83
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:02,204
Root seven is between two and
three, about two point six.

84
00:04:02,204 --> 00:04:05,201
So the left end is about
minus two point six.

85
00:04:05,201 --> 00:04:07,995
Minus three is out, and minus two is in.

86
00:04:08,495 --> 00:04:12,494
Root twenty is between four and
five, about four point five.

87
00:04:12,494 --> 00:04:14,867
So four is in, and five is out.

88
00:04:15,367 --> 00:04:20,203
The integers are minus two, minus
one, zero, one, two, three and four.

89
00:04:20,203 --> 00:04:21,280
Seven integers.

90
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:23,339
Here is your recap.

91
00:04:23,339 --> 00:04:24,838
Screenshot this card.

92
00:04:28,038 --> 00:04:30,874
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

93
00:04:31,274 --> 00:04:32,174
One.

94
00:04:31,769 --> 00:04:36,296
Write the set of x with three less
than or equal to x, and x less than

95
00:04:36,296 --> 00:04:37,934
eight, as an interval.

96
00:04:38,834 --> 00:04:39,734
Two.

97
00:04:39,307 --> 00:04:42,850
What is the length of the
interval from minus two to six?

98
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:44,650
Three.

99
00:04:44,354 --> 00:04:47,305
Why does infinity always
get a round bracket?

100
00:04:48,405 --> 00:04:51,417
Next time: two friends list
their favourite fruits.

101
00:04:51,417 --> 00:04:55,409
All the fruits, the ones they
share, and the ones only she likes.

102
00:04:55,409 --> 00:04:57,958
We meet union, intersection
and difference.
